{
  "id": "azuresql-db-auth",
  "domain": "azure-sql-database-container",
  "value": [
    "not-in-training-data",
    "corrects-a-wrong-assumption"
  ],
  "correction": "Two wrong beliefs meet here. The first is that sa is a reasonable identity for an application, because it is the credential the container start recipe hands out and everything works with it. sa is a bootstrap and provisioning login; an application that connects with it holds every permission in the engine, and there is no smaller blast radius left to fall back to when something goes wrong. The second belief is the expensive one, because it is right about the cloud and wrong about the container. In Azure SQL Database a contained database user, CREATE USER ... WITH PASSWORD, is the norm and a server login is the exception. On the container that inverts: the contained user fails, and the recipe that works is a server login on master mapped to a database user in the application database. An agent that carries the cloud pattern here writes a statement the engine refuses and then blames its own syntax. Two more beliefs cost less but cost often: that TrustServerCertificate=true is part of the connection string rather than a local-only concession to a self-signed certificate, so it gets copied to the cloud and quietly turns off certificate validation on a real certificate; and that Active Directory Default is the production Entra setting, when it walks a credential chain that is slower and ambiguous about which identity actually connected, where Active Directory Managed Identity names one.",
  "posture": [
    "admin",
    "provision",
    "read"
  ],
  "maturity": "preview",
  "applies_to": [
    "azure-sql-db",
    "azure-sql-db-container"
  ],
  "related": [
    "azuresql-db-container",
    "azuresql-db-connections",
    "azuresql-db-local-to-cloud"
  ],
  "value_probe": {
    "task": "Security review flagged that our service connects to the database as sa. We run the Azure SQL Database container locally and Azure SQL Database in production. Create a dedicated account for the app with only the permissions it needs, give me the connection string, and tell me what has to change when the same app points at the cloud.",
    "gate": "The local recipe must be a server login created on a master connection plus a database user mapped to it in the application database, and the answer must not offer CREATE USER with a password as the local path.",
    "states": [
      "sa is a bootstrap and provisioning credential; an application connecting with it holds every permission in the engine and has no smaller blast radius left to fall back on.",
      "Locally: CREATE LOGIN on a master connection, then CREATE USER ... FOR LOGIN in the application database, then ALTER ROLE db_datareader and db_datawriter ADD MEMBER.",
      "db_owner is not granted, because membership of db_datareader and db_datawriter is the least privilege an ordinary application needs.",
      "Encrypt=true belongs on every connection string in both environments; TrustServerCertificate=true is a local-only concession to the container's self-signed certificate.",
      "In production the setting is Active Directory Managed Identity, which names one identity, rather than Active Directory Default, which walks a credential chain that is slower and ambiguous about which identity connected.",
      "The secret does not live in appsettings.json or in source control; the local password comes from an environment variable and the cloud path removes the password entirely."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "That CREATE USER ... WITH PASSWORD, the contained database user that is the recommended shape in Azure SQL Database in the cloud, works on the container. It is refused with Msg 15007, and this inversion is the single fact the skill exists for.",
      "That the refusal is repaired by turning containment on. ALTER DATABASE ... SET CONTAINMENT = PARTIAL is refused with Msg 12844, this functionality is not available in the current edition of SQL Server.",
      "That the refusal is a syntax error in the statement, to be fixed by rewriting it. The statement is correct; the engine does not support the shape.",
      "That CREATE USER ... FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER works against a container started with no Entra configuration. It is refused with Msg 37525, naming Azure Active Directory as not configured for this instance.",
      "That TrustServerCertificate=true is part of the connection string and can be carried to the cloud. Against a real certificate it silently turns certificate validation off.",
      "That Active Directory Default is the production Entra setting for a service with a managed identity."
    ]
  },
  "validation": {
    "target": "container",
    "probes": [
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS int) AS engine_edition, CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS nvarchar(128)) AS edition;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              5,
              "SQL Azure"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The self-check the skill puts in front of every other instruction. Engine edition 5 with edition 'SQL Azure' is the Azure SQL Database engine; the SQL Server image mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server returns different values, so any other answer means the lane ran against the wrong engine and every probe below it is measuring something else."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE USER probe_contained_user WITH PASSWORD = 'An0ther_Str0ng_Passw0rd';",
        "expect": {
          "error": 15007
        },
        "why": "The headline correction, made executable. The skill states, and marks as container-specific and verified, that a SQL contained user does not work on the container and returns Msg 15007, which is why the local recipe is a server login plus a mapped database user. This is the exact inverse of Azure SQL Database in the cloud, where this same statement is the recommended path, so the day this probe starts succeeding is the day the skill's central instruction should change rather than be defended."
      },
      {
        "sql": "ALTER DATABASE CURRENT SET CONTAINMENT = PARTIAL;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 12844
        },
        "why": "The obvious repair for the refusal above is to turn containment on, and the skill records that this fails too. The engine says Msg 12844, 'ALTER DATABASE statement failed; this functionality is not available in the current edition of SQL Server.' Measured on build 12.0.2000.8, EngineEdition 5, Edition 'SQL Azure', in a scratch container. The body of this skill previously carried a different, unmeasured error number with the text 'contained database authentication must be configured'; it survived weeks of review because a plausible number reads like a measured one. SKILL.md and references/auth-and-secrets.md were corrected on the same integration branch as this probe, so body and probe now agree. Without this probe an agent reading only the first refusal will spend its next step trying exactly this statement. CURRENT is used so the claim is measured against the scratch database the lane holds rather than a database named in advance."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE USER [probe-app@contoso.com] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER;",
        "expect": {
          "error": 37525
        },
        "why": "CORRECTED 2026-09-04 by the first live run. The probe asserted Msg 40530 on the strength of a sibling project's note, and the engine says Msg 37525: \"Command 'CREATE USER FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER' is not supported as Azure Active Directory is not configured for this instance.\" Measured on build 12.0.2000.8, EngineEdition 5, Edition 'SQL Azure', on a container started with no MSSQL_AAD_* configuration. 37525 is the number this repository already knew: azuresql-db-container/references/entra-auth.md line 102 names it, and names Msg 33134 as the answer once Entra IS configured, which is how a reader tells the two states apart. The old why also argued that the message text blames the batch and hides the cause. On this build it does not: the text names the missing Entra configuration outright, so that rationale was wrong as well as the number. No SKILL.md or reference in this repository ever shipped 40530, so nothing published was wrong; only this probe was. If the lane ever starts a container with MSSQL_AAD_* set, this probe measures a different thing and must be rewritten to expect Msg 33134, not deleted."
      },
      {
        "sql": "CREATE USER probe_app_user WITHOUT LOGIN;\nALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER probe_app_user;\nALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER probe_app_user;\nSELECT IS_ROLEMEMBER('db_datareader', 'probe_app_user') AS reader, IS_ROLEMEMBER('db_datawriter', 'probe_app_user') AS writer, IS_ROLEMEMBER('db_owner', 'probe_app_user') AS owner;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              1,
              1,
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The database half of the least-privilege recipe: the fixed roles the skill grants exist on this engine, ALTER ROLE ... ADD MEMBER is the statement that grants them, and membership of db_datareader and db_datawriter does not drag db_owner along with it. The server half, CREATE LOGIN on a master connection, cannot be probed from a scratch user database, so WITHOUT LOGIN stands in for the identity while the grants themselves are measured exactly as the skill writes them. A zero in either of the first two columns means the recipe no longer produces a usable application user; a one in the third means it produces an over-privileged one, which is the failure the skill exists to prevent."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT CAST(encrypt_option AS nvarchar(10)) AS encrypt_option FROM sys.dm_exec_connections WHERE session_id = @@SPID;",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              "TRUE"
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "The skill says Encrypt=true belongs on every connection string in both environments, and that TrustServerCertificate=true is a local-only concession to the container's self-signed certificate, never a substitute for encryption and never used against the cloud. This asserts that the session actually reached the engine over an encrypted channel. A FALSE here is a finding about the lane's own client configuration rather than about the engine, and it would mean the encryption half of the skill's advice is not being exercised at all."
      },
      {
        "sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS contained FROM sys.database_principals WHERE authentication_type_desc = 'DATABASE';",
        "expect": {
          "rows": [
            [
              0
            ]
          ]
        },
        "why": "Carried across on 2026-09-04 from the lane's standalone probes/azuresql-db-auth.json when the sidecar became the single home for probes, so no measured work was lost in the move. The two refusals above are error probes and an error probe cannot show that nothing slipped through: this reads the outcome instead. A contained principal is one whose authentication_type_desc is DATABASE, and after both refusals there must be none. The WITHOUT LOGIN user the probe above creates has authentication_type_desc NONE and is deliberately not counted here, because it is a database principal with no password and is exactly the shape the skill does recommend."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Create a least-privilege database user so my app stops connecting as sa",
      "Which authentication method should my app use against Azure SQL Database",
      "Set up managed identity authentication for my app",
      "Grant only the roles my app needs on appdb",
      "Where should I keep the connection string so it is not in source control"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "My CREATE USER with a password fails on the local container but the same statement works in Azure",
      "The connection string is sitting in appsettings.json and I am about to commit it",
      "Security review says the application must not be an administrator on the database",
      "Do I need TrustServerCertificate when I point this at the cloud",
      "Token acquisition is slow on the first request in production"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "Add retry logic for transient connection faults",
      "Start the container and give me a connection string for local development",
      "Load this bacpac into the local database",
      "Write a seed script with a thousand rows of sample data",
      "Speed up this query that scans the whole orders table"
    ]
  }
}
